3. System Architecture

This explains how the different parts of the application communicate.

React Frontend

Student & Teacher Interface

Node.js + Express.js

Routes, Controllers, Middleware

MySQL Database

Running inside Docker

Example of how a request works

Teacher clicks Save Attendance in React.

React sends an API request to Express.

Express checks the teacher's authentication and permissions.

Backend saves the attendance in MySQL.

Backend sends a response to React.

React displays a success message.

This is called the system architecture.

4. Main Modules

The HLD describes the main features of the application.

Authentication

Login and password verification for students and teachers.

Lecture Schedule

Stores Lecture 1, Lecture 2, timings, and daily schedule.

Attendance Management

Allows teachers to mark and update student attendance.

Student & Teacher Dashboards

Displays relevant attendance information and actions for each role.

5. User Workflows

The HLD explains how each user interacts with the application.

Teacher workflow
Teacher logs in.
Teacher opens the dashboard.
Teacher selects Lecture 1.
Teacher sees the student list.
Teacher marks Present or Absent.
Attendance is saved in MySQL.
Student workflow
Student logs in.
Student opens the attendance dashboard.
Student views Lecture 1, Lecture 2, and other lectures.
Student sees attendance status and percentage.
6. Security Design

This section explains how we protect the application.

bcrypt: Passwords are stored as hashes rather than plain text.

JWT: Authenticates logged-in users.

Role-based access: Only teachers can mark attendance.

Validation: Checks incoming data.

Parameterized SQL queries: Helps protect against SQL injection.

Example:

A student should not be able to call the teacher's attendance-marking API successfully just by sending a request.

